      <note class="it-depends" id="note-00002">
        <title>Sample Feature Box Using Lorem Ipsum Postmodern Academia</title>
        <para id="para-00016">
          This is the Description of the “It Depends” FB from the WEG: This feature will present emerging research on complex topics, some of which may potentially be controversial. There should be at least 1 It Depends box in each chapter.
        </para>
        <para id="para-00017">
          If one examines Lyotardist narrative, one is faced with a choice: either reject prestructuralist nationalism or conclude that the significance of the participant is significant form. A number of sublimations concerning Lyotardist narrative may be discovered. “Society is part of the absurdity of reality,” says Foucault. But the characteristic theme of the works of Rushdie is a mythopoetical whole. Marx promotes the use of subcapitalist cultural theory to analyse and read art.
        </para>
        <para id="para-00018">
          However, the example of Lyotardist narrative depicted in Rushdie’s Satanic Verses is also evident in The Ground Beneath Her Feet. Many narratives concerning the genre, and eventually the collapse, of structuralist society exist. It could be said that Marx suggests the use of Lyotardist narrative to attack hierarchy. An abundance of theories concerning subcapitalist cultural theory may be revealed.
        </para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00019">
        As general health concerns increase in middle-age so do those of maintaining brain health and ways to stave off <term id="term-00007">Alzhiemer’s</term> disease and other forms of dementia. Nutrition seems to have constantly varying advice in aiding brain functions. Some reports suggest moderate amounts of caffeine (2-3 cups of coffee a day) are associated with lower risks of developing dementia (Zhang et al., 2021). Higher rates of consumption however have been associated increased rates of dementia and reduced volume of the brain and hippocampus (Pham et al., 2021). Moderate amounts of alcohol (1-2 drinks a day) may have benefits at reducing dementia risk whereas large amounts show increasing risks of dementia when compared to nondrinking (Jeon et al., 2022). 
      </para>
